Kernir patch 0024: the iso9660 reader holds continuation-area passes to the furthest it has read, not where it is

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read_support_format_iso9660.c (struct iso9660, kernir_iso_charge_ce
from 0021, archive_read_format_iso9660_read_header). Applies on top of 0001-0023.

0021 holds the bytes of every continuation area (CE) each record's pass parses to the names allowance plus the bytes
of the image read so far, taken as current_position, since a valid image's distinct areas all lie in those. But
read_header seeks back to a file's body when the directory listing it lies after the body (seekable sources only;
"very unusual", upstream's comment says, yet valid), and current_position then drops to the body's offset. A
directory read after that, at an offset below the furthest point reached, had its areas held to the allowance plus
its own end, though the areas already charged lay up to that furthest point: a valid image could be refused with
"ISO9660 entry names and link targets take more than the names limit" (D1's 24 files with a one-block area each,
then D1's file F, whose body precedes D1, then D2's one-block area: refused under a 1,024-byte names limit).

The reader now keeps the furthest current_position it has reached (kernir_high_water, raised just before that seek,
the only place current_position goes back), and kernir_iso_charge_ce adds the larger of it and current_position.
Without a seek back the two are equal and nothing changes; with one, the bound is still the allowance plus bytes of
the image the reader has passed, at most its size, so a shared chain stays linear in the image's size. register_CE
and read_CE still take areas relative to current_position, and 0021's names charge is untouched.

Apply from Sources/CLibArchive: patch -p1 < patches/0024-iso9660-ce-high-water.patch

--- a/libarchive/archive_read_support_format_iso9660.c
+++ b/libarchive/archive_read_support_format_iso9660.c
@@ -359,6 +359,7 @@
 	uint64_t kernir_entries;	/* Kernir patch 0021 */
 	uint64_t kernir_name_bytes;	/* Kernir patch 0021 */
 	uint64_t kernir_ce_bytes;	/* Kernir patch 0021 */
+	uint64_t kernir_high_water;	/* Kernir patch 0024 */
 	uint64_t volume_size; /* Total size of volume in bytes. */
 	int32_t  volume_block;/* Total size of volume in logical blocks. */
 
@@ -1053,11 +1054,12 @@
  * CPU quadratic in the image's size: each record's pass over each area is
  * charged too, by the area's size, before it is parsed
  * (kernir_iso_charge_ce, a total of its own held to the same allowance plus
- * the bytes read so far, current_position: a valid image's distinct areas,
- * such as xorriso's AAIP ACL and xattr entries, always fit in those, and a
- * shared chain still stays linear in the image's size; refused with the
- * same message). All of these are ARCHIVE_FATAL with
- * their own messages, which Kernir maps to its limits.
+ * the furthest position reached (Kernir patch 0024: current_position, or
+ * where it was before a seek back to a file's body, if that is further): a
+ * valid image's distinct areas, such as xorriso's AAIP ACL and xattr
+ * entries, always fit in those, and a shared chain still stays linear in
+ * the image's size; refused with the same message). All of these are
+ * ARCHIVE_FATAL with their own messages, which Kernir maps to its limits.
  */
 #define KERNIR_ISO_NAME_BYTES 65536
 
@@ -1108,19 +1110,23 @@
 kernir_iso_charge_ce(struct archive_read *a, uint32_t size)
 {
 	struct iso9660 *iso9660 = a->format->data;
-	uint64_t bound;
+	uint64_t bound, reached;
 
 	if (iso9660->kernir_ce_bytes > UINT64_MAX - size)
 		iso9660->kernir_ce_bytes = UINT64_MAX;
 	else
 		iso9660->kernir_ce_bytes += size;
-	/* A valid image's distinct areas lie in the bytes read so far. */
+	/* A valid image's distinct areas lie in the bytes read so far:
+	 * Kernir patch 0024, the furthest the reader has reached, which a
+	 * seek back to a file's body (read_header) does not lower. */
+	reached = iso9660->current_position > iso9660->kernir_high_water ?
+	    iso9660->current_position : iso9660->kernir_high_water;
 	bound = __archive_read_kernir_names_allowance(a,
 	    iso9660->kernir_entries);
-	if (bound > UINT64_MAX - iso9660->current_position)
+	if (bound > UINT64_MAX - reached)
 		bound = UINT64_MAX;
 	else
-		bound += iso9660->current_position;
+		bound += reached;
 	if (iso9660->kernir_ce_bytes > bound)
 		return (kernir_iso_refuse(a, "ISO9660 entry names and link "
 		    "targets take more than the names limit"));
@@ -1551,6 +1557,10 @@
 			iso9660->entry_bytes_remaining = 0;
 			return (ARCHIVE_WARN);
 		}
+		/* Kernir patch 0024: the only place current_position goes
+		 * back; kernir_iso_charge_ce keeps the furthest it reached. */
+		if (iso9660->current_position > iso9660->kernir_high_water)
+			iso9660->kernir_high_water = iso9660->current_position;
 		iso9660->current_position = (uint64_t)r64;
 	}
 
